1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,069
One-to-many is the default shape in a store.

2
00:00:03,069 --> 00:00:05,472
One customer places many orders.

3
00:00:05,472 --> 00:00:08,027
Each order belongs to one customer.

4
00:00:08,027 --> 00:00:10,652
The foreign key lives on the many side.

5
00:00:10,652 --> 00:00:13,180
orders.customer_id.

6
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:16,055
You already met this as a foreign key lesson.

7
00:00:16,055 --> 00:00:17,708
Here we stay with the count.

8
00:00:17,708 --> 00:00:22,777
Many is zero, one, or more, unless you add extra rules.

9
00:00:22,777 --> 00:00:26,125
A customer with zero orders is still a customer.

10
00:00:26,125 --> 00:00:27,958
That is one-to-zero-or-many.

11
00:00:27,958 --> 00:00:34,819
If every customer must have an order, you need a process or a constraint that is harder than a foreign key.

12
00:00:34,819 --> 00:00:36,902
Usually you do not require it.

13
00:00:36,902 --> 00:00:39,569
The one side is required more often.

14
00:00:39,569 --> 00:00:45,333
NOT NULL on orders.customer_id means every order has a customer.

15
00:00:45,333 --> 00:00:48,180
That is one-to-many with a mandatory parent.

16
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:51,263
Optional parent is a nullable foreign key.

17
00:00:51,263 --> 00:00:53,680
Use it when an order can be anonymous.

18
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:57,041
Do not put a list of order ids on the customer.

19
00:00:57,041 --> 00:01:01,291
Lists in a cell break first normal form, and they fight the count.

20
00:01:01,291 --> 00:01:04,986
The many side can grow without rewriting the one side.

21
00:01:04,986 --> 00:01:06,625
That is the whole payoff.

22
00:01:06,625 --> 00:01:08,708
Maya places a third order.

23
00:01:08,708 --> 00:01:10,041
You insert a row.

24
00:01:10,041 --> 00:01:11,916
You do not edit Maya's row.

25
00:01:11,916 --> 00:01:15,000
Cascading deletes are a policy on this shape.

26
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:17,972
If the customer leaves, do the orders leave?

27
00:01:17,972 --> 00:01:19,694
That is the actions lesson.

28
00:01:19,694 --> 00:01:24,402
On the diagram, crow's foot on orders, bar on customers.

29
00:01:24,402 --> 00:01:26,472
Say the sentence both ways.

30
00:01:26,472 --> 00:01:29,847
If both ends say many, you wanted a middle table.

31
00:01:29,847 --> 00:01:33,472
If both ends say one, you wanted the previous lesson.

32
00:01:33,472 --> 00:01:37,944
Join from the one to the many and expect more rows than customers.

33
00:01:37,944 --> 00:01:39,722
That fan-out is correct.

34
00:01:39,722 --> 00:01:42,777
Group when you want one row per customer again.

35
00:01:42,777 --> 00:01:45,375
This shape is ordinary on purpose.

36
00:01:45,375 --> 00:01:50,513
Most honest schemas are a handful of one-to-many arrows around a few hubs.

37
00:01:50,513 --> 00:01:53,180
Hierarchies are one-to-many stacked.

38
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:59,430
A publisher has many authors, an author has many books — if that is true in your store.

39
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:03,388
Sometimes a book has many authors, which is many-to-many.

40
00:02:03,388 --> 00:02:05,763
The stacked one-to-many is a tree.

41
00:02:05,763 --> 00:02:07,833
The many-to-many is a net.

42
00:02:07,833 --> 00:02:11,888
Do not force a net into a tree because trees are easier to draw.

43
00:02:11,888 --> 00:02:15,277
Self one-to-many is a tree in one table.

44
00:02:15,277 --> 00:02:17,972
parent_id on category.

45
00:02:17,972 --> 00:02:19,805
The root has a null parent.

46
00:02:19,805 --> 00:02:23,694
Cycles need a CHECK or a procedure if they would be nonsense.

47
00:02:23,694 --> 00:02:27,236
Aggregates after one-to-many are the usual reports.

48
00:02:27,236 --> 00:02:28,930
COUNT orders per customer.

49
00:02:28,930 --> 00:02:30,680
SUM totals per customer.

50
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:32,666
You already have those verbs.

51
00:02:32,666 --> 00:02:34,944
The relationship is why they work.

52
00:02:34,944 --> 00:02:40,305
Orphan orders after a bad import are many-side rows whose parent is missing.

53
00:02:40,305 --> 00:02:42,402
A foreign key would have stopped them.

54
00:02:42,402 --> 00:02:45,319
If you find them, you found a missing arrow.

55
00:02:45,319 --> 00:02:53,611
Put the arrow on, then clean, or clean, then put the arrow on, in that painful order the data requires.

56
00:02:53,611 --> 00:02:59,291
The usual relationship is usual because commerce is one person, many events.

57
00:02:59,291 --> 00:03:01,222
Learn to see it without thinking.

58
00:03:01,222 --> 00:03:03,847
Then the unusual shapes will stand out.

59
00:03:03,847 --> 00:03:07,041
One box, crow's foot, other box.

60
00:03:07,041 --> 00:03:09,000
Key on the crow's foot side.

61
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:11,875
That is the whole picture, and most of a store.

62
00:03:11,875 --> 00:03:13,222
Count the parents.

63
00:03:13,222 --> 00:03:14,500
Count the children.

64
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:19,541
The children should be greater or equal, except when some parents have none.

65
00:03:19,541 --> 00:03:22,097
That arithmetic is the relationship.

66
00:03:22,097 --> 00:03:26,472
If children outnumber parents by thousands, you still might be right.

67
00:03:26,472 --> 00:03:31,694
If children equal parents always, you might have one-to-one in disguise.

68
00:03:31,694 --> 00:03:32,444
Look.

69
00:03:32,444 --> 00:03:36,944
If children can list two parents in one row, you wanted many-to-many.

70
00:03:36,944 --> 00:03:37,944
Look again.

71
00:03:37,944 --> 00:03:41,736
The usual shape earns its name by surviving that look.

72
00:03:41,736 --> 00:03:43,569
Put the key on the child.

73
00:03:43,569 --> 00:03:45,902
Do not list children on the parent.

74
00:03:45,902 --> 00:03:47,069
Insert to grow.

75
00:03:47,069 --> 00:03:49,375
That is one-to-many when it is healthy.

76
00:03:49,375 --> 00:03:53,069
One-to-many is the default shape of operational data.

77
00:03:53,069 --> 00:03:55,472
One customer places many orders.

78
00:03:55,472 --> 00:03:58,138
One product receives many reviews.

79
00:03:58,138 --> 00:04:00,666
One building contains many rooms.

80
00:04:00,666 --> 00:04:03,250
The many side holds the foreign key.

81
00:04:03,250 --> 00:04:06,625
That sentence is worth repeating until it is muscle.

82
00:04:06,625 --> 00:04:12,458
If you put order_id on customers, you reserved one order slot per customer.

83
00:04:12,458 --> 00:04:15,013
The second order has nowhere to live.

84
00:04:15,013 --> 00:04:22,958
If you put customer_id on orders, every extra purchase is another row with the same customer_id.

85
00:04:22,958 --> 00:04:24,722
That is the implementation.

86
00:04:24,722 --> 00:04:27,486
Cardinality still has two sentences.

87
00:04:27,486 --> 00:04:31,388
A customer has zero, one, or more orders.

88
00:04:31,388 --> 00:04:38,722
An order belongs to one customer, or to one if guests can check out without an account, zero or one.

89
00:04:38,722 --> 00:04:41,680
Optional parent is a NULL foreign key.

90
00:04:41,786 --> 00:04:45,402
Required parent is NOT NULL plus the foreign key.

91
00:04:45,402 --> 00:04:49,083
Crow's foot: the crow's foot sits on the many entity.

92
00:04:49,083 --> 00:04:50,666
The bar sits on the one.

93
00:04:50,666 --> 00:04:55,319
A circle on the one side means a child may exist without a parent.

94
00:04:55,319 --> 00:04:58,124
Read the marks as the English you already wrote.

95
00:04:58,124 --> 00:05:02,041
If the marks and the English disagree, the marks are wrong.

96
00:05:02,041 --> 00:05:07,791
Identifying one-to-many is when the child's key includes the parent: order lines.

97
00:05:07,791 --> 00:05:15,194
Non-identifying is when the child has its own id and merely points: reviews with review_id.

98
00:05:15,194 --> 00:05:19,083
Most application tables are non-identifying one-to-many.

99
00:05:19,083 --> 00:05:20,222
That is healthy.

100
00:05:20,222 --> 00:05:24,208
Do not draw many-to-many because both nouns feel important.

101
00:05:24,208 --> 00:05:28,527
If one side is clearly the parent of a list, it is one-to-many.

102
00:05:28,527 --> 00:05:33,749
Students in a section, lines on a receipt, payments on an invoice.

103
00:05:33,749 --> 00:05:36,569
Lists with a single owner are one-to-many.

104
00:05:36,569 --> 00:05:45,833
Implement, then query: FROM orders JOIN customers ON orders.customer_id equals customers.customer_id.

105
00:05:45,833 --> 00:05:49,069
The join follows the key you placed on the many side.

106
00:05:49,069 --> 00:05:53,916
If the join needs a third table already, you may have many-to-many hiding.

107
00:05:53,916 --> 00:05:56,874
Check the English before you invent the junction.

108
00:05:56,874 --> 00:06:05,374
Practice: write both sentences, place the foreign key, mark NOT NULL if the parent is required, name the constraint.

109
00:06:05,374 --> 00:06:07,374
That is a complete one-to-many.

110
00:06:07,374 --> 00:06:10,527
Pretty crow's feet without the column are theater.

111
00:06:10,527 --> 00:06:14,555
Orders and customers, both sentences, then SQL.

112
00:06:14,555 --> 00:06:17,722
A customer has zero one or more orders.

113
00:06:17,722 --> 00:06:22,888
An order has one customer in this store, because guests get a guest customer row.

114
00:06:22,888 --> 00:06:28,194
Foreign key orders.customer_id NOT NULL references customers.

115
00:06:28,194 --> 00:06:33,055
Insert customer one, then two orders with customer_id one.

116
00:06:33,055 --> 00:06:38,083
SELECT from orders where customer_id equals one returns two.

117
00:06:38,083 --> 00:06:39,319
That is the many.

118
00:06:39,319 --> 00:06:47,374
SELECT from customers join orders should not put customer columns on the many grain if you then count customers as orders.

119
00:06:47,374 --> 00:06:48,569
Watch the grain.

120
00:06:48,569 --> 00:06:51,680
Crow's foot on orders, bar on customers.

121
00:06:51,680 --> 00:07:00,374
If guest checkout allows NULL customer_id, circle on customers, and the second sentence becomes zero or one.

122
00:07:00,374 --> 00:07:02,777
The column changes with the sentence.

123
00:07:02,777 --> 00:07:09,611
Teachers and students in a single section is one-to-many if each student has one homeroom teacher.

124
00:07:09,611 --> 00:07:14,944
If students have many teachers, you already left this lesson for many-to-many.

125
00:07:14,944 --> 00:07:17,222
Stay until the list has one owner.

